course: dictionary
lesson: 13132
title: Understanding foolishness
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Foolishness is a noun. It is a name for silly or unwise behaviour or thinking, done without good sense. «The boy's foolishness nearly got him lost in the snow.» The clues are nearly and lost in the snow: he did something unwise, and it could have been dangerous.
explain: Foolishness can also mean nonsense. «Stop this foolishness!» Here someone is not calling a person stupid. They mean the silly fooling about should stop now. Ask yourself what is happening around the word, then try your meaning in the sentence to see if it fits.
explain: Foolishness is the opposite of wisdom, which means good judgement. Old stories often set them side by side, such as the wise man who built his house on the rock and the foolish man who built his on the sand.
fact: Foolishness is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say some foolishness, not a foolishness.
fact: «Foolishness» means silly or unwise behaviour or thinking, without good sense.
fact: «Foolishness» can also mean nonsense, as in «Stop this foolishness!»
fact: «Sheer foolishness» means foolishness of the most complete kind, with no good sense at all.
fact: Foolishness is often the opposite of wisdom, and some foolishness can be dangerous, such as playing near railway lines.
exercise: «Climbing onto the roof in the storm was sheer foolishness.» What does foolishness mean here? | *Silly, unwise behaviour without good sense | A kind of weather | Careful planning and good judgement | A quiet, calm talk
exercise: Mum says, «Stop this foolishness!» What does foolishness mean here? | *Nonsense and silly fooling about | A tidy room | A wise and careful plan | A long, sad story
exercise: In the old story, which man shows foolishness? | The man who built his house on the rock | The man who planted a garden | *The man who built his house on the sand | The man who asked the king for advice
test: Which word is the opposite of foolishness? | Silliness | *Wisdom | Mistake | Nonsense
test: Playing near railway lines is an example of what? | Good judgement | Careful planning | *Foolishness that can be dangerous | A kind of sport
exam: Which sentence best shows foolishness? | Sam read a book quietly | *Sam jumped onto the frozen lake even though the ice was cracking | Sam helped to tidy the classroom | Sam checked the weather before the trip
